//? Title: Discovering a Gaussian graph with PC-stable
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Simulate two independent causes of a shared downstream variable. Fit a
partially directed graph and use conditional independence tests to see
how a collider changes the relationship between its parents.
)

> Recover the collider from observations

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pc_stable.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
    namespace causal = datamunge::causal;
    datamunge::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> data(3500, 5);
    std::mt19937_64 rng(41);
    std::normal_distribution<double> normal;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < data.rows(); ++i) {
        data(i, 0) = normal(rng);
        data(i, 1) = normal(rng);
        data(i, 2) = data(i, 0) + data(i, 1) + normal(rng);
        data(i, 3) = data(i, 2) + normal(rng);
        data(i, 4) = .9 * data(i, 0) + normal(rng);
    }
    const auto fit = causal::pc_stable(data, {.alpha = .001});
    const auto marginal = causal::gaussian_ci_test(data, 0, 1);
    const auto conditional = causal::gaussian_ci_test(data, 0, 1, {2});
    std::cout << "Partial correlations before and after conditioning: "
              << marginal.correlation << " and " << conditional.correlation
              << ". Independence tests: " << fit.tests << ".\n";
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    fit.diagram({{0, 3}, {0, -3}, {5, 0}, {10, 0}, {5, 5}},
                {"Cause A", "Cause B", "Collider", "Downstream", "A's neighbor"})
        .save_svg("build/pc_stable.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Partial correlations before and after conditioning: -0.0145577 and -0.515408. Independence tests: 36.
\image(figures/pc_stable.svg)
// result_end

The simulated arrows are A and B into Collider, Collider into Downstream,
and A into its neighbor. The last edge can stay undirected in the learned
graph because its direction is ambiguous within the observational
equivalence class. The search assumes independent Gaussian observations,
causal sufficiency, acyclicity and faithfulness. The test cutoff and
conditioning depth can affect the recovered graph.
